{"id":"amd-earnings-q3-2026","title":"AMD earnings — Advanced Micro Devices, Q3 2026","section":"Earnings","date":"2026-11-03","html":"<p>Management sees AI chip demand running ahead of what it can build well into next year, and the thing that could spoil it is a shortage of memory and other parts that caps shipments and eats margin.</p>\n<p>Reported 2026-11-03. Next report: not found. Updated 2026-11-12. Research, not investment advice.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-short-version\">The short version</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Demand:</strong> stronger. Data Center demand is above supply, and AMD's own AI chips and racks began shipping in volume in Q3.</li><li><strong>Guidance:</strong> raised. Q4 revenue about $17B, plus or minus 3%, against a Q3 guide of about $13B. The 2027 view went up to revenue growth of more than 80%. Gross margin guide is one point lower, about 55%.</li><li><strong>Biggest worry:</strong> the memory and parts shortage. It limits how much AMD can ship and it is already shaving the margin.</li><li><strong>Chart:</strong> leans up, not yet confirmed. Price is back over the 50-day but stopped under my 61.8% line at $560.57. A close above it confirms; a close below $500.05 flips it.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"the-numbers\">The numbers</h3>\n<ul><li>Revenue $13.38B, up 45%, against an estimate of $13.09B. Data Center revenue $8.55B, up 97%.</li><li>GAAP EPS $1.46 against a $1.42 estimate; non-GAAP EPS $2.09. The estimate is from Webull and analysts usually estimate the adjusted figure, so that EPS comparison mixes GAAP and adjusted. Treat it as rough.</li><li>Non-GAAP gross margin 56%. Q3 revenue landed above the top of the guided range ($13B, plus or minus $300M).</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-said-about-the-road-ahead\">What management said about the road ahead</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Next quarter:</strong> revenue about $17B, plus or minus 3%, roughly 66% above a year ago. Non-GAAP gross margin about 55%.</li><li><strong>2027:</strong> revenue growth of more than 80%, server revenue roughly doubling, Data Center above 80% of total revenue.</li><li><strong>Product ramp:</strong> MI450 AI chips and Helios racks shipped in volume in Q3; deployments are expected to rise substantially in Q4. AI chip revenue should pass server processor revenue for the first time in Q4.</li><li><strong>Supply:</strong> demand exceeds supply. AMD is co-investing with wafer, substrate, packaging and memory suppliers to get more.</li><li><strong>Margins:</strong> the AI mix lowers the margin percentage while gross profit dollars grow. Higher memory and component costs are being shared with customers.</li><li><strong>PCs:</strong> unit shipments are expected to fall by a double-digit percentage in 2027 because of memory and component costs.</li><li><strong>In her words:</strong> &quot;We delivered a record quarter with revenue exceeding the high end of our guidance.&quot; Lisa Su, Chair and CEO.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-is-worried-about\">What management is worried about</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Memory and parts shortage.</strong> An industry-wide memory shortage is named in the filing. The margin guide is already down a point (56% to 55%), and it is the reason given for the PC unit decline in 2027.</li><li><strong>Commitments made to secure supply.</strong> Purchase obligations are $48.8B, with $33.4B due through fiscal 2027, plus growing prepayments. Inventory is $11.5B. These only pay off if the demand shows up on schedule.</li><li><strong>A handful of very large customers, with strings attached.</strong> OpenAI and Meta each hold warrants for up to 160M shares, which add earnings swings and future dilution. AMD also guarantees some partners' data center leases.</li><li><strong>China.</strong> Export licences for AI chips sold to China, and Chinese countermeasures.</li><li><strong>One manufacturer.</strong> Reliance on TSMC for its chips.</li><li><strong>A shrinking PC market.</strong> A double-digit fall in PC units next year means the growth rests on Data Center alone.</li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"the-big-worry-the-memory-and-parts-shortage-both-ways\">The big worry: the memory and parts shortage, both ways</h3>\n<p><strong>What would ease it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Memory price gains keep slowing. TrendForce put DRAM contract prices up about 20% in the fourth quarter, after about 80% in the second quarter and 40% in the third (Oct 20). Micron also expects more moderate increases after its current quarter.</li><li>New memory plants arrive. Micron's new sites in Idaho, New York, Japan, Taiwan and Singapore come online between 2027 and 2030.</li><li>AMD's co-investments with memory, substrate and packaging suppliers turn into reserved supply.</li><li>Customers keep accepting shared cost increases, so the pressure stays a one-point margin matter and not more.</li><li>Fewer PCs and phones built next year leaves a little more memory for servers. TrendForce says PC DRAM supply could fall in 2027 as capacity shifts to servers (Sep 30).</li></ul>\n<p><strong>What would make it worse</strong></p>\n<ul><li>The shortage runs longer than planned. Micron says supply will be much tighter in 2027 and 2028 than in 2026 and that it cannot see when DRAM supply and demand balance.</li><li>The memory that sits beside AI chips gets dearer. Micron has sold the vast majority of its 2027 high-bandwidth memory at significantly higher prices, and a TrendForce headline puts the 2027 blended price for that memory up 121% from 2026.</li><li>Bigger buyers lock it up first. Nvidia's supply and capacity commitments reached $279B, mostly memory. AMD's purchase obligations are $48.8B.</li><li>TSMC's leading-edge wafers and packaging stay short. TSMC says AI demand is still above its supply.</li><li>Customers stop sharing the cost, and the margin guide slips again.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>How this company is placed against it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Pricing: AMD says it is sharing higher costs with customers, and its margin guide gave up one point. Nvidia, Broadcom and Dell each named the same memory drag, so this is the industry's problem, not AMD's alone.</li><li>Scale: AMD is the smaller buyer. Its $48.8B of purchase obligations compares with Nvidia's $279B of commitments, so in a scramble for memory it is not first in line.</li><li>Balance sheet: it raised $4.75B of senior notes in August and holds $11.5B of inventory. Long-term supply contracts of its own for memory: not found.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"money-plans\">Money plans</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Capital spending:</strong> $648M in Q3. The larger money is going into supplier prepayments and purchase commitments ($48.8B).</li><li><strong>Buybacks and dividend:</strong> no buybacks in Q3; $9.2B of authorization left. No dividend found.</li><li><strong>Debt:</strong> $4.75B of senior notes issued in August.</li><li><strong>Deals:</strong> two acquisitions pending: Taalas (about $2.9B) and World Labs (about $8.2B in stock).</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"people-and-governance\">People and governance</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>CEO:</strong> Lisa Su, President and CEO since October 2014 and Chair since February 2022. Not a founder; she joined AMD in January 2012.</li><li><strong>CFO:</strong> Jean Hu, EVP, CFO and Treasurer. She was also interim Chief Accounting Officer until Emily Ellis took that job on Dec 15, 2025.</li><li><strong>Successor named:</strong> not found. No succession plan turned up in the filings and pages opened.</li><li><strong>Chair/CEO split:</strong> no. Su holds both. Nora Denzel is Lead Independent Director.</li><li><strong>Control:</strong> no dual-class shares or controlling holder found in the pages opened. Watch the warrants instead: OpenAI and Meta can each take up to 160M shares.</li><li><strong>Recent changes or investigations:</strong> director Joe Householder (on the board since 2014, audit chair) retired Aug 19, 2026; the filing says there was no disagreement. Tim Ryan of Citi joined the same day and KC McClure took the audit chair. Jon Olson did not stand for re-election in May. A stockholder proposal to make special meetings easier to call failed in May. In June the board set Su's long-term award target at $36M, 75% tied to performance. Investigations or senior executive departures: none found.</li><li><strong>How much rests on one person:</strong> a lot. Su has run the company for twelve years and holds both top seats, with no named successor. You would learn about a succession problem only when it happened.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"does-the-chart-agree\">Does the chart agree?</h3>\n<figure class=\"chart\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" data-ticker=\"AMD\" viewBox=\"0 0 760 400\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"AMD · daily · my levels (not yours) chart\" font-family=\"Arial, sans-serif\"><text x=\"8\" y=\"18\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#20242C\" class=\"c-ink\">AMD · daily · my levels (not yours)</text><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"374.0\" y2=\"374.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"289.0\" y2=\"289.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"204.0\" y2=\"204.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"119.0\" y2=\"119.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"34.0\" y2=\"34.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"10.3\" x2=\"10.3\" y1=\"247.4\" y2=\"268.6\" stroke=\"#2F6E4A\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-up\"/><rect x=\"8.9\" y=\"253.1\" width=\"2.9\" height=\"6.5\" fill=\"#2F6E4A\" class=\"c-up\"/><line x1=\"14.9\" x2=\"14.9\" y1=\"235.1\" y2=\"318.3\" stroke=\"#A63A2E\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-dn\"/><rect x=\"13.5\" y=\"237.8\" width=\"2.9\" height=\"33.4\" fill=\"#A63A2E\" class=\"c-dn\"/><line x1=\"19.6\" x2=\"19.6\" y1=\"268.9\" y2=\"304.6\" stroke=\"#A63A2E\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-dn\"/><rect x=\"18.1\" y=\"280.5\" width=\"2.9\" height=\"1